@prefix sh:        <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix rdfs:      <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix ag:        <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix sosa:      <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix market:    <http://example.org/orexis/market#> .
@prefix actuation: <http://example.org/orexis/actuation#> .
@prefix ssn:        <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix ssn-system: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/systems/> .

#  THE ACTIONS THIS PACKAGE BRINGS — one node each, and the whole of one (knowledge/domain/action.md):
#    ag:means      the kind of act, a kernel word;
#    ag:available  its PRECONDITION — a SELECT over the world binding ?property ?via ?direction,
#                  and ?for_agent where the row is a duty owed to someone; `$me` and
#                  `$properties` substituted by the collector, prefixes prepended by the store;
#    sh:construct  its EFFECT, with ag:retracts, ag:landsAfter and ag:confirmedBy beside it;
#    ag:takenBy    the capability whose module carries it out.
#  Precondition, effect and taker were three files and a fourth surface; a STRIPS operator is one
#  thing, and an author adds a way of acting by adding a node here and a `take()` to a module.

#################  What BUYING would make true  #################
#
#  The lever every plant in this project actually pulls, and the planner could not see it. Only
#  sensing and actuation shipped effects, so a search over a plant's menu saw Observe alone,
#  correctly found that looking does not wet soil, marked the plan PARTIAL and deferred to the
#  reflex — which is what waters the society. Measured the hour #265 landed: `better` was zero
#  across three societies and `blind` was 1 for every plant. The planner decided correctly and
#  could not see the one lever that mattered. See #268.
#
#  THE HONEST DIFFICULTY, stated rather than smuggled: **an agent does not control whether it
#  WINS.** A dose is an act whose effect follows from taking it; a bid is an act whose effect
#  follows from taking it AND from a host matching it AND from nobody outbidding it. So this
#  rule predicts the world in which the bid clears, which is optimistic, and the optimism is
#  the correct choice for three reasons rather than a convenience:
#
#    * It is what planning does everywhere. A STRIPS action schema states what the action
#      achieves, not what it achieves times a probability; uncertainty lives in re-planning and
#      in monitoring, which is where this repo already puts it.
#    * The machinery for the unmet case already exists and already runs. An Acquire that does
#      not clear produces an UNMET verdict through the same expectation path a dose that never
#      landed does — see `an-intention-is-an-amortised-deliberation` on the verification arc.
#      A rule that hedged here would be inventing a second, quieter way to be disappointed.
#    * A pessimistic rule is not more honest, it is just useless: predicting no change would
#      make Acquire indistinguishable from doing nothing, and the search would go on refusing
#      to bid — which is exactly the bug this closes.
#
#  What it must NOT do is hide that. The optimism is why `ag:landsAfter` below is generous and
#  why `ag:confirmedBy` is by OBSERVATION: nothing is true until a reading says so.

market:Acquiring
    a ag:Action ;
    ag:means ag:Acquire ;

    #  AVAILABLE in a market I bid in WHOSE WINNINGS CAN PHYSICALLY REACH MY SUBJECT: the host
    #  holds an actuator plumbed to my own pot, so opening it puts the good where I am. Deduced
    #  link by link; only the last — that the good RAISES this property — is stated, once, by
    #  the domain, because it is physics. The valuation joins through the venue's good (#198):
    #  property alone put both directions on both rows the day two venues priced one property.
    ag:available """
        SELECT DISTINCT ?property ?via ?direction WHERE {
          VALUES ?property { $properties }
          $me ag:actsFor ?subject .
          $me market:bidsIn ?via .
          ?via market:marketFor ?src .
          ?src market:supplies ?good .
          ?host market:hosts ?via ;
                actuation:hasActuator ?lever .
          ?lever actuation:actuates ?subject .
          ?valuation market:ofGood ?good ;
                     market:aboutProperty ?property ;
                     market:direction ?direction .
        }""" ;

    #  WHO CARRIES IT OUT — the module whose family this names (knowledge/domain/actor.md).
    ag:takenBy market:Bidding ;
    rdfs:label "what winning a bid makes true" ;

    #  THE SAME ARITHMETIC AS A DOSE, and that is the point rather than a coincidence. Water
    #  bought and water poured raise the same property by the same amount; what differs is who
    #  owns the valve and whether anyone had to be paid. So the prediction is `value + litres /
    #  conversion` — the one function `actuation:ActuateEffect` exists to keep in a single
    #  place — reached along the plumbing walk `affordances.rq` already deduces the row from:
    #  a venue I bid in, whose source supplies a good, whose host holds a lever plumbed to MY
    #  subject. An agent bidding in a market no valve connects to its pot gets no row and no
    #  effect, because a delivery that cannot reach you is, for you, no lever at all.
    #
    #  The conversion is the agent's OWN belief, keyed by the term the venue tie names — the
    #  copy it would lose money by being wrong about, and the copy a review would revise.
    sh:construct """
        CONSTRUCT {
            ?obs a sosa:Observation ;
                 sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest $subject ;
                 sosa:observedProperty $property ;
                 sosa:resultTime ?now ;
                 sosa:hasSimpleResult ?predicted .
        } WHERE {
            $me ag:actsFor $subject ; market:bidsIn ?via .
            ?via market:marketFor ?source .
            ?source market:supplies ?good .
            ?host market:hosts ?via ; actuation:hasActuator ?lever .
            ?lever actuation:actuates $subject .
            ?term market:ofGood ?good ; market:aboutProperty $property .
            GRAPH $beliefs { $me ?term ?conversion }
            FILTER(?conversion > 0)
            #  AN ACT OF ZERO SIZE MAKES NOTHING TRUE, and the filter is load-bearing rather
            #  than defensive. `value_bid` cedes at or above the aim, so a content agent sizes
            #  its bid at nothing — and without this the rule would still CONSTRUCT a reading
            #  equal to the value it started from. In a world that holds no reading yet, that
            #  fabricates one: the search then scores the step as an improvement, because
            #  ending ignorance IS an improvement (#137), and a content plant would buy water
            #  to find out how wet it is. Yielding nothing instead leaves the world untouched,
            #  the signature unchanged, and the step discarded as somewhere already seen —
            #  which is the same road every other "this does not help" arrives by.
            FILTER($litres > 0)
            BIND($value + ($litres / ?conversion) AS ?predicted)
            BIND(BNODE() AS ?obs)
            BIND(NOW() AS ?now)
        }""" ;

    #  WHEN IT LANDS — and this is where buying and dosing genuinely differ, so the number is
    #  built from what a BIDDER can see rather than from what a host knows.
    #
    #  Two legs are visible and one is not. `market:redeemWindowS` is on the VENUE, so a bidder
    #  reads it: it is how long a winner has to present, and using the whole window rather than
    #  a guess at how promptly this agent will present is deliberate — an upper bound is the
    #  safe direction here, because patience shorter than an effect's landing time lets an agent
    #  act before it could know (#250). The host's valve then pours, and that leg is the
    #  identical arithmetic `actuation:ActuateEffect` uses, off the same public device facts.
    #
    #  WHAT IS EXCLUDED, and cannot honestly be included: the time until a round opens and
    #  closes. `market:bidWindowS` and `market:roundCooldownS` are the HOST's private beliefs —
    #  a bidder is not told them, and must not be, because a venue that published its schedule
    #  would let a bidder time its arrival. So this is the landing time of a bid that finds a
    #  round already open. A bid that has to wait for one lands later than this says, and the
    #  honest name for that gap is the convening seam
    #  (`the-lot-is-the-hosts-standing-offer`), not a number to invent here.
    ag:landsAfter """
        SELECT ?seconds WHERE {
            $me market:bidsIn ?via .
            ?via market:redeemWindowS ?window ; market:marketFor ?source .
            ?host market:hosts ?via ; actuation:hasActuator ?lever .
            ?lever actuation:actuates $subject ;
                   actuation:maxDoseMl ?cap ;
                   actuation:mlPerSecond ?rate .
            FILTER(?rate > 0)
            BIND(IF($litres * 1000 < ?cap, $litres * 1000, ?cap) AS ?ml)
            BIND(?window + (?ml / ?rate) AS ?seconds)
        }""" ;

    #  BY OBSERVATION, and for a sharper reason than the dose's. A claim is co-signed and a
    #  valve reports that it opened, so it is tempting to call this confirmed by report. But a
    #  report says the SUPPLIER acted; only a reading says the water reached this pot. That gap
    #  is exactly `an-unconfirmed-dose-is-not-a-delivered-one` seen from the buyer's side, and
    #  the buyer is the one who paid.
    ag:confirmedBy ag:ByObservation ;

    #  The reading it replaces, identical to the dose's and for the identical reason: the sensed
    #  graph holds one node per (subject, property), so a predicted reading left beside the old
    #  one would put two results on one node and a reader would take whichever it found first.
    #  Retracts NOTHING when it adds nothing, for the same reason and by the same guard: a
    #  zero-size act that removed the standing reading would leave the world blinder than it
    #  found it, which is the opposite of the error above and just as wrong.
    ag:retracts """
        CONSTRUCT { ?obs ?p ?o } WHERE {
            GRAPH $sensed {
                ?obs sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest $subject ;
                     sosa:observedProperty $property ;
                     ?p ?o
            }
            FILTER($litres > 0)
        }""" .

#################  What SERVING a presented claim makes true  #################
#
#  The rule #255 was missing, and the mirror of Acquire's: a host that HONOURS discharges the
#  debt and drains its vessel by the amount the record says it owes. The PREMISE is the vessel
#  holding enough — `?level >= ?litres` — and a premise that does not hold binds NOTHING, so
#  serving from a dry vessel predicts no change, the world keeps its parent's signature, and
#  the search discards the step as somewhere already reached. That silence is the whole
#  mechanism: the step that IS reachable from there is Acquire, whose effect raises the very
#  level this premise reads — so "refill, then serve" emerges as a two-step plan from two
#  rules that never mention each other, which is what a-plan-is-a-path-of-graph-diffs promised
#  chaining would be.
#
#  THE AMOUNT HAS ONE SOURCE: the obligation record, written when the claim was issued
#  (`ag:amountL`). An obligation recorded before the amount existed simply never binds here —
#  unplannable, still servable by the honoured row, which is the graceful arrival every
#  widening in this repo is held to.

market:Serving
    a ag:Action ;
    ag:means ag:Apply ;

    #  AVAILABLE as a DUTY — ?for_agent bound — for each buyer this venue admits who acts for a
    #  subject my valve serves: the ACL's shape applied to obligation, so a presentation from
    #  anyone else is a forgery before it is a duty. The valuation is tied by the RECIPIENT's
    #  stated need, not the good alone (#198 from the other side): a valve serving a pot must not
    #  also claim to move that pot's stored litres. Disclosure, not a filter — what an obligation
    #  makes me do arrives as a want, and the deliberator ranges over it like any other.
    ag:available """
        SELECT ?property ?via ?direction ?for_agent WHERE {
          $me market:hosts ?venue ; actuation:hasActuator ?via .
          ?venue market:marketFor ?source .
          ?via actuation:drawsFrom ?source ;
               actuation:actuates ?subject .
          ?source market:supplies ?good .
          ?for_agent market:bidsIn ?venue ; ag:actsFor ?subject .
          ?subject ssn-system:hasOperatingRange/ssn-system:inCondition/ssn:forProperty ?property .
          ?valuation market:ofGood ?good ;
                     market:aboutProperty ?property ;
                     market:direction ?direction .
        }""" ;

    #  WHO CARRIES IT OUT — the module whose family this names (knowledge/domain/actor.md).
    ag:takenBy market:Hosting ;
    rdfs:label "what serving a presented claim makes true" ;

    sh:construct """
        CONSTRUCT {
            ?obl ag:dischargedAt ?now .
            ?obs a sosa:Observation ;
                 sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest ?vessel ;
                 sosa:observedProperty ?levelProp ;
                 sosa:resultTime ?now ;
                 sosa:hasSimpleResult ?left .
        } WHERE {
            GRAPH $owed { ?obl a ag:Obligation ; ag:forClaim $claim ; ag:amountL ?litres .
                          FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?obl ag:dischargedAt ?done } }
            $me market:hosts ?venue ; actuation:hasActuator ?lever .
            ?lever actuation:drawsFrom ?vessel .
            ?vessel ssn-system:hasOperatingRange/ssn-system:inCondition/ssn:forProperty ?levelProp .
            GRAPH $sensed { ?cur sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest ?vessel ;
                                 sosa:observedProperty ?levelProp ;
                                 sosa:hasSimpleResult ?level }
            FILTER(?level >= ?litres)
            BIND(?level - ?litres AS ?left)
            BIND(BNODE() AS ?obs)
            BIND(NOW() AS ?now)
        }""" ;

    #  WHEN IT LANDS: the valve's own open-seconds for the recorded amount, the identical
    #  arithmetic the dose and the bid state, off the same public device facts — a deadline
    #  computed anywhere else would be a second claim about when the world should answer.
    ag:landsAfter """
        SELECT ?seconds WHERE {
            GRAPH $owed { ?obl a ag:Obligation ; ag:forClaim $claim ; ag:amountL ?litres }
            $me market:hosts ?venue ; actuation:hasActuator ?lever .
            ?lever actuation:maxDoseMl ?cap ;
                   actuation:mlPerSecond ?rate .
            FILTER(?rate > 0)
            BIND(IF(?litres * 1000 < ?cap, ?litres * 1000, ?cap) AS ?ml)
            BIND(?ml / ?rate AS ?seconds)
        }""" ;

    #  BY OBSERVATION, from the host's own side: the valve reports that it opened, but what
    #  this rule PREDICTS is the vessel's level, and only the next reading of it says the
    #  water left. The buyer's side confirms itself on the buyer's own arc, exactly as
    #  AcquireEffect argues from the other end of the same pipe.
    ag:confirmedBy ag:ByObservation ;

    #  The vessel reading it replaces — and ONLY when the premise held, by the same guard:
    #  a dry vessel must not lose its standing reading to a serve that never happened.
    ag:retracts """
        CONSTRUCT { ?obs ?p ?o } WHERE {
            GRAPH $owed { ?obl a ag:Obligation ; ag:forClaim $claim ; ag:amountL ?litres .
                          FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?obl ag:dischargedAt ?done } }
            $me market:hosts ?venue ; actuation:hasActuator ?lever .
            ?lever actuation:drawsFrom ?vessel .
            ?vessel ssn-system:hasOperatingRange/ssn-system:inCondition/ssn:forProperty ?levelProp .
            GRAPH $sensed { ?obs sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest ?vessel ;
                                 sosa:observedProperty ?levelProp ; ?p ?o .
                            ?obs sosa:hasSimpleResult ?level }
            FILTER(?level >= ?litres)
        }""" .
